# Copy to .env.local (Neon `main` branch) and .env.test (Neon `test` branch).
# Both are gitignored — only this example file is committed.
# Later tasks add their own keys here (Twilio, Inngest, Clerk).

# The role the app and the tests connect as: `app_user`, which owns no tables, so
# Postgres RLS actually binds it. Written for you by `npm run db:app-user` — paste
# the owner's connection string into MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL (or DATABASE_URL on a
# first run) and let the script derive this one.
DATABASE_URL=

# The Neon owner role. Owns the tables, bypasses RLS, and is used by nothing but
# drizzle-kit — migrations have to create tables, which app_user cannot.
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=

# Stripe, test mode. The **platform** account's secret key — never a connected
# account's: charges are created on the restaurant's account by passing
# `{ stripeAccount }` per request, which is what makes them direct charges.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The signing secret for the endpoint, from `stripe listen --forward-to
# localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook` in dev, or the endpoint's own secret in the
# dashboard. Not interchangeable between the two — a dev secret against a dashboard
# endpoint fails every signature with no other symptom.
#
# The dashboard endpoint must be registered as a **Connect** webhook on the platform
# account. That is what populates `event.account`, which the handler checks against
# `locations.stripe_account_id` before it will mark an order paid. Registered on a
# connected account instead, `account` is absent and every flip becomes a logged no-op.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Both may be left blank for a pay-at-pickup-only deployment: `stripe()` is lazy, so
# nothing reads them until a card order is placed. The suite never reads them at all
# (it mocks the SDK whole), but `.env.test` still needs placeholder values because
# `env()` throws on a missing var.

# The owner's order SMS (Task 10's fan-out), over Twilio's REST API. A trial account
# and one number are enough for dev — trial accounts may only text *verified* numbers,
# so a seeded restaurant phone has to be verified in the console or every send is a
# 21608. Production is gated on A2P 10DLC registration, which takes days: file it
# before it is the thing holding a launch up.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_FROM_E164=

# Deliberately **absent from `.env.test`**, unlike the Stripe placeholders above. The
# suite mocks `sendSms` whole, so a test that reaches this code has forgotten to — and
# with no credentials it fails loudly on `env()` instead of posting to Twilio for real.
# A placeholder here would turn that mistake into a network call from CI.

# Where the owner's SMS points them: the kitchen feed. One URL for the platform today;
# it becomes per-location when the feed is behind a slug.
FEED_URL=

# Our phone — the number every alarm escalates to. In E.164, like every other number
# in this system (`sendSms` refuses anything else).
#
# It is the *second* phone to ring, never the first: a stale order texts the restaurant
# six minutes in and only reaches here four minutes after that, because the owner can
# fix it in one tap and we cannot cook. A printer that has stopped polling texts both
# at once — that one is often ours to chase.
#
# Read per alert rather than at import, so a deploy with it unset fails the alerting
# step and only the alerting step. Blank in `.env.test`: every alerting test stubs it,
# so a test that reaches the real value has forgotten to mock `sendSms` and fails
# loudly instead of texting a real person from CI.
OPS_ALERT_E164=

# Clerk, and the thing worth knowing is that neither of these is the tenancy boundary.
# The mapping that decides which restaurant a session may open is
# `organizations.clerk_org_id` in our own database (see `src/lib/authz.ts`); Clerk only
# says who is signed in and which of their orgs is active. A Clerk org with no row
# here can see nothing, which is the correct default for a new signup.
#
# The application needs **Organizations enabled** — without them every session has a
# null `orgId`, `requireLocationAccess` refuses every request with a 403, and the feed
# is a blank refusal page with no error anywhere to explain it.
#
# Both may be left blank in `.env.test`: the route tests mock `auth()` whole and never
# reach Clerk. Blank in `.env.local` means nobody can sign in, so the feed is unusable —
# which is louder than a half-working one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# Inngest. Local dev needs neither — `npx inngest-cli dev` discovers `/api/inngest`
# and runs the whole thing without an account. Both are required in the cloud:
# INNGEST_EVENT_KEY signs events we send, INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY verifies the requests
# the orchestrator makes back to us. Read by the SDK itself, not through `env()`.
INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=
INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=
